EXT. FLOATING TEAHOUSE - LANTERN MARSH - DUSK

A raft-house on stilts drifts between reeds, paper lanterns glowing. Steam curls from a copper kettle. Six figures sit around a low table, each cup full, each smile polite.

KAELITH (30s, silver-braided elf, green sash) pours for MOTHER ORVETH (70s, a gnarled herbalist with moss in her hair) while BRAM STONEFIST (40s, dwarf warrior, bandaged hand) tears a rice cake in two, young PIP THISTLEWICK (teens, apprentice thief) pockets a spoon, the scholar DOMINIQUE ASHVALE (50s, ink-stained fingers) turns the pages of a ledger, and the ferrywoman SUN-HEE (60s, bamboo hat) hums at the tiller without ever looking at the passengers.

                    KAELITH
          (to the table)
          A toast. To the road behind us.

                    BRAM
          To the road behind us.

                    PIP
          To the road behind us!

                    KAELITH
          And to the road ahead.

                    BRAM ^
          And to the road ahead.

                    DOMINIQUE
          (quietly, to the ledger)
          There are six names in this book. We are six.

                    MOTHER ORVETH
          Then the book is accurate.

                    DOMINIQUE
          It is dated a hundred years ago.

The humming stops. Sun-Hee lifts her head and, for the first time, looks at each of them in turn. Kaelith lowers her cup, Bram's hand drifts toward his axe, Pip freezes mid-pocket, Dominique closes the ledger softly, and Mother Orveth smiles the way a river smiles at a stone.

                    SUN-HEE
          The ferry does not return. It only arrives.

The lanterns go out one by one, and the water beneath the raft is no longer water.
